# @public — sadish allocation seam: the ONE place every other module draws memory from.
#
# alloc.cyr — `sd_alloc(n)` is what surface.cyr, geom.cyr, path.cyr, raster.cyr, present.cyr
# and error.cyr call instead of the stdlib `alloc(n)`. By default it IS `alloc(n)`; a consumer
# may install a hook with `sd_alloc_set(fp)` and every byte sadish asks for while the hook is
# set comes from the hook instead.
#
# ⭐ WHY A SEAM EXISTS AT ALL. `lib/alloc.cyr` is a bump allocator with NO `free()`. Before 0.5.5
# every fill cost the global heap a fixed 327,824 B and every stroke of a closed 4-vertex rect
# 2,789,016 B (MEASURED, 8x8 canvas, one call each) — permanently, per call. A per-frame consumer
# (dhancha's frame arena, and crab on top of it, whose headline gate asserts a rendered frame
# costs the heap EXACTLY 0 bytes) had no way to draw scalable text through sadish without
# leaking a canvas plus a path per glyph per frame — the defect dhancha filed as
# `2026-09-13-scalable-text-allocates-per-call-outside-the-frame-arena.md`. Routing sadish's
# allocations onto the consumer's arena is the ONLY way a frame can cost the heap nothing,
# and the consumer is the only party that knows the frame boundary. Hence: a hook.
#
# THE CONTRACT.
#   · The hook applies to EVERYTHING sadish allocates while it is set — surfaces, canvases,
#     coverage buffers, clip masks, paths, verb/point arrays, points, matrices, error records,
#     flatten mid-points, the PPM/presenter buffers. sadish does not distinguish "the result"
#     from "the working set"; a consumer scopes the hook around the work whose lifetime it is
#     choosing: `var prev = sd_alloc_set(&mine); ...draw...; sd_alloc_set(prev);`.
#   ⛔ A pointer that outlives the hook's backing store is dangling. If the hook is an arena
#     that gets reset every frame, NOTHING sadish returned under it may be kept across the reset
#     — not a path, not a canvas, not a surface. That is the consumer's lifetime call, exactly as
#     it already is for `dh_falloc`.
#   · A hook returning 0 is handled precisely as `alloc()` returning 0 already is: the callers
#     that check (sd_surface_new, sd_canvas_new, sd_path_new, sd_path_grow) return 0 /
#     SADISH_ERR_OOM; the callers that do not check never did. No new failure path.
#   · `sd_alloc_set` returns the PREVIOUS hook so a nested scope unwinds in order; 0 restores the
#     global allocator. `sd_alloc_get` reads it back.
#   ⚠ The fill/stroke scratch in raster.cyr is deliberately NOT on this seam — it is allocated
#     once for the life of the process from the global `alloc` (see `_sd_scratch_init`; the
#     SD_AA_AREA accumulator in coverage.cyr and the styled-stroke batch in stroke.cyr likewise),
#     and since 0.6.1 GROWN there too when a path outgrows it. A hook pointing at an arena that
#     gets reset would otherwise leave the scratch dangling on the second frame.
#   ⚠ The hook is a process-wide global, like the scratch: sadish has never had a lock anywhere
#     and is not thread-safe. Set it from the thread that draws.

# The installed hook: fn(n): ptr (0 on failure), or 0 = the global allocator.
var _sd_alloc_fn = 0;

# @public — allocate n bytes for sadish through the consumer's hook, or the global allocator when
# none is set. Returns 0 on failure, as `alloc` does.
fn sd_alloc(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_alloc_fn == 0) { return alloc(n); }
    return callptr(_sd_alloc_fn, n);
}

# @public — install `fp` (`fn(n): ptr`, 0 on failure) as the allocator for everything sadish
# allocates from now on. Returns the PREVIOUS hook so a caller can scope it (set, draw, restore).
# Passing 0 restores the global allocator.
fn sd_alloc_set(fp): i64 {
    var prev = _sd_alloc_fn;
    _sd_alloc_fn = fp;
    return prev;
}

# @public — the current hook; 0 = the global allocator.
fn sd_alloc_get(): i64 {
    return _sd_alloc_fn;
}
